  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A cold front moves through the region late \ntoday into tonight, bringing rain at times through the day.\n\nThe warm front associated with the main surface low will \nbriefly move northward into the region early this afternoon, \nbut will be quickly followed by a cold front late today into\nthis evening.\n\nLow level flow will keep an onshore component through much of\nthe day. As such, low level moisture is expected to increase\nthrough mid day, with surface dew points rising well into the \n60s and precipitable water values increasing to near 2 inches. \nPrecipitable water values that high are well above the 90th \npercentile for this time of year. \n\nHowever, with little instability, there will generally be a lack\nof embedded convection within the areas of rain. Instead, this\nwill primarily be a more steady, stratiform rain event. As a\nresult, the risk for excessive rainfall leading to flash\nflooding continues to be low. Given the high PWATs, there may \nstill be some pockets of moderate rainfall at times, \nparticularly for areas northwest of I-95. But the risk for \nflooding remains low (less then 5 percent) across the entire \nregion.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A stationary front south of the region could keep \nrain chances around, especially for Delmarva through mid week.\n\nThe front will be south of the region on Monday and remain \nthere through mid week. Confidence is not the highest pertaining\nto all aspects of sensible weather, namely precipitation. How \nfar south that front sags south will be a big factor in how much\nrain we see through the week. The further south it stalls, the \nless likely our region will see continued rain chances through \nthe mid week period. At least for Tuesday, multiple models\ntrended slightly further north with the front, and as such, rain\nis likely on Tuesday generally for areas along and south of the\nPA Turnpike/195 corridors.\n\nRegardless, our region will be on the cool side of the front \nthrough this period keeping temperatures below normal. \nConfidence is higher here. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...A closed mid-level low ejecting from the Ohio Valley \ncould help to spin up surface low pressure off our coast by \nThursday, potentially bringing another chance for rain.\n\nAs a mid level low digs in from the northwest, models are showing \nsurface cyclogensis by Wednesday off the Carolinas. There remain\nsignificant differences in the track and strength of the low. \nThe closer the track is to the coast, the more likely our region\nwill see rain and gusty winds on Thursday and Friday. Further\ncomplicating the picture, now multiple models depict the low\ninitially progressing northeast before retrograding westward\ntowards the coast at the end of the week. All that to say, the\nrain and wind forecast from Thursday onward remain very\nuncertain at this time.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 4...There will be an increasing risk for \ncoastal/tidal flooding through the second half of the week.\n\nRegardless of the exact track of the coastal low, it looks as \nif we will be in persistent onshore flow from at least Tuesday \nonward for the Atlantic Coast, Delaware Bay, and Raritan Bay. \nThis long of an onshore flow component increases the risk for \ntidal flooding, especially for back bays. For the later half of \nthe week, the track, strength of the low, and fetch will all \nplay a role with tidal departures. In addition, the full moon is\non Saturday, meaning astronomical high tides are increasing as \nwe get towards the end of the week.\n\nIt is still uncertain, but we could see minor tidal flooding as\nearly as the late Wednesday tide cycle. \n\nFor the Chesapeake Bay, the expected wind direction tends to\nlimit tidal flooding concerns, but it will need to be watched if\npersistent easterly flow at the mouth of the Chesapeake.",
